Z||000004||GTFiero1||10-19-2001||05:58 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com" TARGET=_blank>www.v8archie.com</A>  and <A HREF="http://www.thefibersmith.com" TARGET=_blank>www.thefibersmith.com</A>   the fiber smith has a 308 conversion and a F40 conversion coming out soon. The Mera is not a kit car. It is a Replica built in a factory new and sold through pontiac dealers. If you can get ahold of one that would be to your advantge since they'll hold together well and ever panel matches up. theres some times some on ebay motors in Ferarri and Kit Cars a Mera would be the best choice your you want a quality built replica of a 308<P>------------------<BR>Adam J. B.||||reg||1
Z||000005||ferrari_momo||10-20-2001||11:00 PM||||jfire,<BR> ur in VA?  i might be moving there in a year or so.  hope to get a nice sized shop there too.  but i think it is very expensive there.  hey are you good with mechanical?  i am selling my v8 308 for $6,500 as is.  no time at the moment, and i want to get money to invest in other things for my shop.<BR>pat k. <A HREF="http://www.thefibersmith.com" TARGET=_blank>www.thefibersmith.com</A>  <P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||rogergarrison||10-21-2001||05:45 AM||||As a starter car, Id find a 308/328 kit. The body kits can be found for a couple thousand occasionaly on Ebay and you can find a decent running Fiero donor for $1,000-1500. Only on those body kits are they strictly a bolt on proposition. You just take off a Fiero panel and bolt on the Ferrari. After you have the body, its up to you and your wallet how far you want to take it. I did a lot of engine mods, 75% Ferrari interior, Ferrari wheels, all the right emblems, etc. You dont have to do any frame mods. Any other kits requires a lot more.<P>------------------<BR>         <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=11878523&p=52990885"> ||||reg||1
